### 5.2 First a check: tonight's measurements match Nov 16

Before comparing anything new, the two methods of Nov 16 were run again unchanged, to see whether tonight's machine
behaves as it did then.

<<T:controls>>

The Nov 16 values below were computed again from that night's raw records with tonight's script, so both nights
are measured in the same way.

- With no tenant, decode's mean token gap: 12.68 ms on Nov 16, 12.63 ms tonight (median 12.01 against 11.98 ms).
- Usual tenant, pause after the step: decode's mean, +6.3 % on Nov 16 and <<V:c1_fix:mean>> tonight; the p99 of single
  steps, +24.0 % against <<V:c1_fix:s99>>.
- Tenant compiled as a whole, pause after the step (the best method of Nov 16): decode's mean, +4.2 % then and
  <<V:c2a:mean>> tonight; the p99 of single steps, +10.7 % against <<V:c2a:s99>>.
- Repeats of one setting differ by less than 0.2 points in the mean. So differences of 0.5 points or more in a mean
  can be trusted below; for the p99 of single steps a difference needs 3–4 points to count.

### 5.3 How much less decode slows once the tenant is changed

Four tenants, all pausing after the step, each with the budget that gives a tenant progress of about 3,500 tokens per
second: 50 GB per second for the usual tenant, 30.7 GB per second for the tenant with only the output layer changed,
19.2 GB per second for the new tenant; the tenant compiled as a whole keeps its setting of Nov 16 (the same cap on
progress as the usual tenant, 3,712 tokens per second).

<<T:tenants>>

![tenants and ways of pausing](../coloc_adjust/report/figures/night_methods.png)

*Figure 5 — the six settings of this section and the next side by side. Each bar is the change against tonight's runs
with no tenant; where a setting was run more than once, the circles are the single runs. The last column is the tenant's
progress.*

Row by row:

1. **Only the output layer changed (change one).** Decode's mean hardly moves (<<V:c1_fix:mean>> → <<V:v_step:mean>>),
   but the p99 of single-step time falls from <<V:c1_fix:s99>> to <<V:v_step:s99>>. This confirms the inference of
   section 2: **the slowest 1 % of decode steps were the ones that met the output-layer segments**. Problem B is
   answered.
2. **The new tenant (all three changes).** Decode's mean falls to <<V:cvfm_step:mean>>, almost half of the usual
   tenant's; the TPOT p90 per request falls from <<V:c1_fix:tpot90>> to <<V:cvfm_step:tpot90>>.
3. **Against the best method of Nov 16 (whole-model compilation)**: decode's mean is a little lower
   (<<V:cvfm_step:mean>> against <<V:c2a:mean>>), not by much. The clear gains are elsewhere: a lower p99 of single
   steps (<<V:cvfm_step:s99>> against <<V:c2a:s99>>), and about <<V:gain_tok_all:cvfm_step>> more training over the
   whole 11 minutes (<<V:cvfm_step:tok_all>> against <<V:c2a:tok_all>> tok/s). The reason for the latter was not worked
   out. The logs show that both tenants were stopped for lack of GPU memory for only about 2 seconds, so it is not
   "one can still train and the other has to stop"; the difference arises in the phases with many requests, where
   every step of both tenants gets slower and those of the tenant compiled as a whole more so (1.86 against 1.66 seconds
   per step on average over the run).

One thing is not explained: the tenant with only the output layer changed and the tenant compiled as a whole move about
the same data per second when measured alone (about 30 GB), yet decode's mean cost differs (<<V:v_step:mean>> against
<<V:c2a:mean>>). So "data moved per second" explains most of the differences but not all. A possible reason is that the
data a tenant moves next to decode is not what it moves alone (section 7); that was not measured tonight.

### 5.4 Do slices help

Same tenant, same budget, only the way of pausing differs: compute a whole step and then pause, or pause briefly every
time "budget × 12 ms" of data has been moved.

<<T:slices>>

- **The tenant loses almost no progress any more.** For the new tenant: in the phases counted here,
  <<V:cvfm_step:tok>> tok/s with the pause after the step and <<V:cvfm_w12:tok>> with slices, the same; over the whole
  11 minutes <<V:cvfm_step:tok_all>> against <<V:cvfm_w12:tok_all>> tok/s, about 2 % less with slices. The old slices of
  Nov 16 lost 9 % in the counted phases alone. The "lost progress" half of problem A is solved.
- **Decode's steps are affected more evenly.** For the new tenant: the median single-step time rises from
  <<V:cvfm_step:s50>> to <<V:cvfm_w12:s50>> and the p90 falls from <<V:cvfm_step:s90>> to <<V:cvfm_w12:s90>>; the two
  meet. That is what slices are for: no group of decode steps that is hit hard while the rest is not hit at all.
- **The mean does not change** (<<V:cvfm_step:mean>> against <<V:cvfm_w12:mean>>). Slices spread the same total effect;
  they do not shrink it.
- **No visible gain for the TPOT per request** (p90: <<V:cvfm_step:tpot90>> against <<V:cvfm_w12:tpot90>>). A request
  has 128 steps, so unevenness between single steps averages out inside a request anyway.

So the full answer to problem A: slices now work as designed and lower the p90 of decode's single steps by about 2
points; they do not lower the mean and do not help request-level figures. Use them if the worst gap between tokens
matters; if only the TPOT per request matters, pausing after the step is enough.

