### 5.6 How many SMs for the tenant

On Nov 16 the tenant was given 32 SMs. The new tenant is much lighter, so 16 and 64 were tried again. Same budget
(19.2 GB per second), slices.

<<T:sms>>

- Decode's mean cost is almost the same for the three SM counts.
- The share of time the tenant computes differs more than twofold (<<V:cvfm_w12_sm16:comp>> on 16 SMs,
  <<V:cvfm_w12_sm64:comp>> on 64). So **decode's cost follows the data the tenant moves, not how many SMs it uses or how
  long it computes**.
- On 16 SMs the tenant cannot keep up with its budget and loses about 7 % of its progress. 32 SMs stay a good choice;
  there is no reason to change it.

### 5.7 Letting the budget follow decode's measured slowdown

The budgets so far were fixed by hand. Another way (already there on Nov 16) is to give a target, for example "decode
at most 5 % slower": every 2 seconds the scheduler looks at how much slower decode actually is, lowers the tenant's
budget if it is too slow and raises it otherwise. Tonight this was joined with the new tenant and the new rule for
pausing.

<<T:slo>>

![automatic budget](../coloc_adjust/report/figures/night_slo.png)

*Figure 7 — The six settings of the table above. Each bar is the change against tonight's runs without a tenant; where
a setting was run more than once the dots are the single runs. The last column is the tenant's progress.*

