## 6. Conclusions

Answering the three problems of the start.

**C. Which part of a tenant step moves the data?** Measured: 55 % is the output layer and the loss, four blocks of
7.97 GB each that cannot be paused and that peak near the card's limit. This measurement became the basis of everything
after it.

**B. What caused the slowest 1 % of decode steps?** Those four blocks. With the output layer computed over blocks of the
vocabulary, the p99 of decode's single-step time goes from <<V:c1_fix:s99>> above the no-tenant value to
<<V:v_step:s99>>; with the new tenant and slices it is <<V:cvfm_w12:s99>>.

**A. Why did slices not help, and now?** They did not because the slicing code assumed all segments were equal while
they differ 70-fold, and the four largest could not be cut at all. Now pauses follow the data actually moved and the
tenant can pause everywhere, and slices work as designed: almost no lost progress (about 2 % over the whole 11 minutes), and the p90 of decode's single steps about
2 points lower. They do not lower decode's mean and do not help the TPOT per request.

**For the main line (the tenant keeps training, decode slows as little as possible) the most useful result of the night
is the three changes to the tenant:**

| What is compared | Usual tenant, pause after the step (the method of Nov 16) | New tenant, slices |
|---|---|---|
| Decode's mean slowdown at the same tenant training (about 3,500–3,700 tok/s) | <<V:c1_fix:mean>> | <<V:cvfm_w12:mean>> |
| The same, TPOT p90 per request | <<V:c1_fix:tpot90>> | <<V:cvfm_w12:tpot90>> |
| The same, p99 of decode's single-step time | <<V:c1_fix:s99>> | <<V:cvfm_w12:s99>> |
| Tenant training when decode is a little over 6 % slower | <<V:c1_fix:tok>> tok/s | <<V:cvfm_w12_x2:tok>> tok/s |
| Budget set automatically, level "target 5 %" (decode's mean in fact about 2 % slower): how much the tenant trains | <<V:c1_slow5:tok>> tok/s | <<V:cvfm_slow5_w12:tok>> tok/s |
| Data per step, tenant alone | 57.2 GB | 21.9 GB |
| GPU memory needed with the same way of splitting a step (4 batches of 2) | 5.0 GiB | 2.3 GiB |

Against the best method of Nov 16 (the tenant compiled as a whole) the new tenant is only a little better in decode's
mean (<<V:c2a:mean>> → <<V:cvfm_w12:mean>>). The progress is mainly: a smaller tail of decode's single steps, about
<<V:gain_tok_all:cvfm_w12>> more tenant training over the whole 11 minutes, and a place to pause after every layer, so
that slices and the automatic budget can be used with it.

**One more conclusion, about where the cost comes from**: at one budget, with the tenant on 16, 32 or 64 SMs, decode's
mean cost is almost the same. Decode's cost follows the data the tenant moves. This supports the overall direction of
controlling that data once more.

