## 7. What did not work, and what to read with care

- **Leave 3–4 points of margin on any p99.** Between tonight's no-tenant baselines the p99 of single steps itself
  differs that much. Means, medians and p90 do not have this problem (they differ by less than 0.2 points).
- **The single-step statistics leave out two kinds of gaps** (those while another request is admitted, and the engine's
  own occasional double gaps); see 5.1. With nothing left out, the p99 of token gaps is set mainly by the engine itself
  and changes by about 1 % with or without a tenant; the tables also give the p95 with nothing left out.
- **The data per segment was measured with the tenant alone.** Whether it is the same next to decode was not measured
  tonight; it was used as "about the same". The unexplained difference of 5.3 may come from this.
- **Only the arrival rates of 0.5 and 1 request per second are counted.** At higher rates decode queues by itself and
  the tenant often stops for lack of GPU memory; that was not analysed separately.
- **The new tenant does not support the ways of splitting a step that keep part of the data in host RAM.** When GPU memory is very tight it can
  only pause. It needs less GPU memory to begin with, and under tonight's load it trained more over the 11 minutes than
  the usual tenant; more extreme memory pressure was not tried.
- **The cautious automatic setting (5.7) was left as it is.**
- **Four runs are not used for conclusions** and are listed in the table at the end of this section: one was cut
  13 seconds before its end; one was made while the new tenant still used the usual tenant's table of memory needs (it
  needs less than half of that memory; with the old table it took the larger ways of splitting for too large and
  computed 73 % of its steps as "4 batches" and 25 % as "8 batches" instead of the "2 batches" that would have fitted); one was made while the new tenant still used "1 batch of 8" (63 % of its steps), which moves more data; one was
  made before the range of the automatic budget was changed.
- **Whether the training is the same was checked as far as "the loss agrees step by step over 1,500 steps"** (3.4).
  The tenant trains on randomly generated token sequences; it is a stand-in job that produces load. So what is compared
  is numerical agreement, not how good the final model is. A training on real data run to its end was not compared.
- **A problem found and fixed on the way**: the per-layer compilation at first compiled only 8 layers (PyTorch's limit
  on recompilations) without any message; it showed only in "the data did not change".
- One comparison was started tonight on a program that imitates decode instead of vLLM; it was stopped half-way and
  none of it is used in this document.

